body: "## Problem\n\n`--all-release-tags --release-version-from-package --date-from-version` names the window **after the latest tag** after `package.json`'s version. Between releases, `package.json` still holds the version that was just tagged and published, so items that close after that release are rendered **inside the already-published release's section**. `CHANGELOG.md` on main then claims that the published npm version contains changes it does not have. When an item lands in a section that used to be item-less, that section's dependency notes also disappear, because item-less sections keep Dependencies and sections with items drop them. So regenerating rewrites the content of a section that was already released.\n\n## Observed in the fleet (2026-11-12)\n\n- **pm-github#233, after merging release v2026.11.12.** The released section `## 2026.11.12 - 2026-11-12` held only `### Dependencies` (Bump jscpd #232). On the PR branch the same heading now also lists the PR's own pending item under `### Fixed`. To keep the dependency line, the agent added `--dependency-updates` to the repo's generator.\n- **pm-context#225, a closure PR.** `package.json` is still 2026.11.1, a prepared release that never published. The 11-12 fix is rendered as `## 2026.11.1 - 2026-11-01`, and that section's ten dependency entries are gone.\n\n## Expected\n\n- When the package version already has a matching release tag (`v<version>` or `<version>`), the window after that tag renders as `## Unreleased`, or is omitted, not as another `## <version>` section, and the tagged section's content stays exactly what the tag window produces.\n- `--release-version-from-package` names the pending window only when that version is **not** tagged yet, which is the release-preparation case.\n- `--check` stays stable across a release and the following closure regenerations, so they never rewrite a published section.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- In a fixture whose tag `v2.0.0` has one dependency-only commit, with `package.json` 2.0.0 and one item closed after the tag: the output has `## Unreleased` with the item, and `## 2.0.0` keeps only its dependency line.\n- With `package.json` 2.0.1 untagged (preparation), the same item renders under `## 2.0.1 - <date>`.\n- The released section's bytes are unchanged between the two runs.\n\n\n---\n\n### GitHub comments (2)\n\n> **@coderabbitai[bot]** (2026-11-12T22:23:25Z)\n>\n> <!-- coderabbit-ai-generated:v1 -->\n> \n> <!-- This is an auto-generated issue plan by CodeRabbit -->\n> <details>\n> <summary>🔗 Related PRs</summary>\n> <dl>\n> <dd>\n> <br>\n> \n> unbraind/pm-changelog#333 - Keep changelog checks stable after empty release tags [closed]\n> </dd>\n> </dl>\n> </details>\n> \n> ---\n> <details>\n> <summary>📝 Issue Planner</summary>\n> <dl>\n> <dd>\n> <br>\n> \n> <sub>Check the box below or use the `@coderabbitai plan` command to generate an implementation plan and prompts that you can use with your favorite coding assistant.</sub>\n> \n> - [ ] <!-- {\"checkboxId\":\"8d4f2b9c-3e1a-4f7c-a9b2-d5e8f1c4a7b9\"} --> Create Plan\n> </dd>\n> </dl>\n> </details>\n> \n> \n> ---\n> <details>\n> <summary> 🧪 Issue enrichment is currently in open beta.</summary>\n> <dl>\n> <dd>\n> <br>\n> \n> \n> You can configure auto-planning by selecting labels in the issue_enrichment configuration.\n> \n> To disable automatic issue enrichment, add the following to your `.coderabbit.yaml`:\n> ```yaml\n> issue_enrichment:\n>   auto_enrich:\n>     enabled: false\n> ```\n> </dd>\n> </dl>\n> </details>\n> \n> 💬 Have feedback or questions? Drop into our [discord](https://discord.gg/coderabbit)!\n\n> **@unbraind** (2026-11-16T20:05:07Z)\n>\n> More fleet evidence, 2026-11-16, and a second facet: an item whose `release` field names an old version.\n> \n> Nine closure PRs from the 2026-11-14 in-progress sweep close items that were delivered in an already published release. The items carry that release assignment, so `--respect-item-release` renders them inside the published section even after merging current main, where `package.json` already names a newer pending version. Each time, the published section loses its Dependencies bullets, because \"sections with items drop Dependencies\":\n> \n> | PR | Published section | Bullets removed / added |\n> |---|---|---|\n> | pm-linear#223 | 2026.11.11 | −5 / +1 |\n> | pm-ts-starter#211 | 2026.11.10 | −5 / +1 |\n> | pm-starter#209 | 2026.11.9 | −4 / +1 |\n> | pm-todos#199 | 2026.11.11 | −6 / +1 |\n> | pm-presets#218 | 2026.11.10 | −4 / +2 |\n> | pm-jira#207 | 2026.11.5, 2026.11.7 | −1 / +2 |\n> | pm-memory#70 | 2026.11.13 | bullet order reversed |\n> | pm-hypothesis#32 | 2026.10.25 (on npm, untagged) | +1 |\n> | pm-swarm#76 | 2026.11.11 | −2 |\n> \n> `changelog:check` demands exact generator output, so a repository cannot keep the published notes by hand. Every late closure of a delivered item therefore either rewrites a released section or never closes. These items are stuck in `in_progress` until this is fixed.\n> \n> Proposal, so that published sections stay stable:\n> 1. A section's Dependencies list depends only on the commits in its tag window, never on whether the section also has items. A section that gains a late-closed item keeps its dependency bullets.\n> 2. To avoid churning every existing item-bearing section on the upgrade, apply (1) only to sections where a dependency bullet would otherwise disappear. Alternatively, ship (1) behind an opt-in flag that the fleet enables with one regeneration per repository.\n> 3. Contract test: closing an item assigned to a tagged release adds exactly its own bullet and removes nothing.\n>"
